== pre-registered verdict (#256)
   AUC(met vs hemco) on byte follow-through = 0.651  (n=283 vs 334)
     (note: the 0.7 bar is undemanding — a uniform shift of one unit clears it at 0.719)
   Spearman rho per class/arm (train on one arm, test on another — the agreed rule).
     A value only counts toward the verdict when its arm has >= 8 scored handles AND >= 4
     distinct values on BOTH axes: n=3 with a two-way tie is monotone by construction, not a finding.
     mean_abs_gap_blocks     met/a=+0.635  met/b=+0.634  hemco/a=+0.271  hemco/b=+0.266   <- |rho|>=0.5 on 2+ QUALIFYING arms
     max_abs_gap_blocks      met/a=+0.624  met/b=+0.627  hemco/a=+0.235  hemco/b=+0.254   <- |rho|>=0.5 on 2+ QUALIFYING arms
     frac_large_gap          met/a=+0.221  met/b=+0.198  hemco/a=+0.061  hemco/b=+0.064
     mean_read_kib           met/a=+0.084  met/b=+0.110  hemco/a=-0.150  hemco/b=-0.122
     frac_monotonic          met/a=-0.017[feature has 3 distinct]  met/b=+0.031[feature has 3 distinct]  hemco/a=+0.039  hemco/b=+0.069
     frac_straddle           met/a=-0.034  met/b=-0.033  hemco/a=+0.023  hemco/b=-0.056

   best |rho| = 0.635 (mean_abs_gap_blocks); threshold 0.5
   VERDICT: SEPARATION — a feature of a handle's first reads predicts byte follow-through.
            Validate it out-of-sample (fit one arm, test another) before designing a policy on it.

